        wh: 'WHY',
        pointer: null,
        q: 'Why is the Monarch butterfly orange and black — what does this colouring warn other animals about?',
        a: 'This is called warning colouration (aposematism). Monarch caterpillars eat milkweed, which contains toxins called cardenolides. These toxins accumulate in the butterfly\'s body and make it taste terrible — even poisonous — to birds. The bright orange and black pattern is a visual warning: \"I am toxic, do not eat me.\" Birds that try one quickly learn to avoid all Monarchs. The pattern essentially advertises its own unpalatability.',
