        q: 'What is X-chromosome inactivation, and how does it produce the specific patch pattern seen in calico cats?',
        a: 'In each cell of a female embryo, one X chromosome is randomly \"switched off\" (inactivated) — a process called lyonisation. Each cell then replicates with that inactivation pattern. Because orange-producing genes are on the X chromosome, patches where the orange-X is active become orange; patches where it is inactive become black. The random spatial distribution of these early inactivation events determines the unique, never-repeating mosaic pattern of every calico cat — making each one\'s coat a visible record of its early cellular development.',
