        q: 'When did the domestic cat genome diverge from its wildcat ancestor, and what does this reveal about the domestication process?',
        a: 'Genomic studies (Driscoll et al., 2007; Ottoni et al., 2017) show cat domestication began ~10,000 years ago but progressed extremely slowly — cats remained genetically very similar to their wildcat ancestors far longer than dogs. Unlike dogs, which were deliberately bred for specific traits, cats were essentially self-domesticated: wildcats that tolerated humans reproduced more successfully near settlements, with minimal human selection. This \"commensal domestication\" pathway is unique among major domestic animals.',
