        a: 'Many veterinary behaviourists and animal welfare scientists argue yes. Breed-specific welfare legislation exists in some jurisdictions (e.g., Danish laws require working breeds to have access to activities that meet their natural behavioural needs), but is rare. The alternative — relying on owner education and voluntary welfare standards — is demonstrably insufficient given high relinquishment rates of Border Collies from unsuitable homes. A framework requiring buyers of recognised high-drive breeds to demonstrate knowledge of their needs and suitable living conditions (similar to licensing requirements for certain exotic animals) would reduce welfare harms without restricting ownership per se.',
